INSERT INTO cases(case_id,case_number,title,court_id,case_type,cause,judgment_date,parties,summary,facts,reasoning,holding,ruling,outcome,keywords) VALUES ('case_wh_d75add1d','(2023) E 01 Civil First-Instance No. 14718','Qinghe Dormitory Intermediary Service Fee Contract Dispute Case','court_wh_intermediate','Contract Dispute','Housing lease contract dispute','2023-05-09','Tenant Lin, Landlord Luo','Qinghe Dormitory Intermediary Service Fee Dispute: After the contract was not successfully concluded, the intermediary collected the full service fee; the disputed amount was approximately 5071 yuan.','After the lease relationship at Qinghe Dormitory was established, a dispute arose over the intermediary collecting the full service fee after the contract was not successfully concluded. The intermediary was unable to provide complete records of the services rendered, and the tenant and landlord subsequently presented evidence separately regarding the written attachments and actual losses.','In hearing the dispute over an intermediary service fee at Qinghe Dormitory, the collegial panel first verified the service agreement and property-viewing records, then compared the contract appendices with the notice records. Based on the evidence currently available, when the services have not been fully performed, the fee should be determined in light of the actual workload, and liability should be allocated according to the actual impact.','The key point of the judgment is that when services have not been fully performed, fees should be determined based on the actual amount of work completed; in the Qinghe dormitory rental, the deposit, accessibility facilities, and repair deadline cannot be based solely on oral statements.','The Qinghe Sushe agency service fee matter was ultimately handled in accordance with “refunding service fees that were not actually incurred.” Upon calculation, the supported amount was 4245 yuan; the portion exceeding the scope of the evidence was not supported.','Partial support','Wuhan, rental, agency service fee, deposit');
INSERT INTO cases(case_id,case_number,title,court_id,case_type,cause,judgment_date,parties,summary,facts,reasoning,holding,ruling,outcome,keywords) VALUES ('case_wh_e93d4ae4','(2025) E0102 Civil First Instance No. 22419','Qinghe Yating early lease-termination contract dispute case','court_wh_jiangan','Contract Dispute','Housing lease contract dispute','2025-07-04','Tenant Zhou, Landlord Xu','Qinghe Yating early lease-termination dispute: The tenant requested early termination because of changed living conditions; the disputed amount is approximately 35134 yuan.','Regarding the Qinghe Yating property, the parties confirmed that the tenant requested early termination due to changes in living conditions. Because the tenant sent written notice promptly after discovering the issue, the parties were unable to agree on how the deposit, repair, or service costs would be allocated.','The contract attachments, communication emails, and time records in the Qinghe Yating case file corroborate one another. On that basis, the court held that liquidated damages may be adjusted in accordance with the law when they are excessively high and the losses are limited, and that the parties'' promises not written into the contract should be subject to strict scrutiny.','In the Qinghe Yating case, the court clarified that when liquidated damages are excessively high and losses are limited, they may be adjusted in accordance with the law, and that key fees and living conditions should be documented through contract appendices, handover forms, or notice records.','The court ruled regarding Qinghe Yating: appropriate liability shall be borne according to the actual losses; the established losses shall be assessed at 13425 yuan for purposes of liability, and the parties shall otherwise continue performing the contract as agreed.','Partial support','Wuhan, Early Lease Termination, Service Fee');
INSERT INTO cases(case_id,case_number,title,court_id,case_type,cause,judgment_date,parties,summary,facts,reasoning,holding,ruling,outcome,keywords) VALUES ('case_wh_099cb7fd','(2025) E 0111 Civil First-Instance No. 13934','Qinghe Chunxiao Identity Materials Contract Dispute Case','court_wh_hongshan','Contract Dispute','Housing lease contract dispute','2025-02-07','Tenant Gu, landlord Han','Qinghe Chunxiao identity materials dispute: Before signing the contract, the intermediary requested excessive photographs of identification documents, with the disputed amount approximately 28175 yuan.','This case arose from an identity-documentation issue after the handover of Qinghe Chunxiao. The tenant submitted materials claiming that the agent had requested too many photos of identity documents before the contract was signed, while the landlord explained its handling of the matter by stating that “the agent was unable to provide complete records of the service process.”','For the identity-document issue at Qinghe Chunxiao, the court relied primarily on the information-collection page and chat records, considered the handover condition and subsequent remedial measures, and held that rental verification should follow the principles of specified purpose and minimum necessity.','The Qinghe Chunxiao case confirmed that rental verification should follow the principles of clear purpose and minimum necessity; the issues concerning door width, repairs, and service fees should all be assessed based on specific written evidence.','Regarding the Qinghe Chunxiao dispute, the court ordered the deletion of irrelevant materials and an end to further processing. The amount supported by documentation was determined to be 3082 yuan, and the remaining claims will be handled separately.','Mediation','Wuhan, rental, identity documents, service fee');
INSERT INTO cases(case_id,case_number,title,court_id,case_type,cause,judgment_date,parties,summary,facts,reasoning,holding,ruling,outcome,keywords) VALUES ('case_wh_aafd3aec','(2024) E 0106 Civil First Instance No. 2047','Qinghe Heyuan Written Attachment Contract Dispute Case','court_wh_wuchang','Contract Dispute','Housing lease contract dispute','2024-07-24','Tenant Chen and landlord Wu','Qinghe Heyuan written attachment dispute: The parties disputed an oral promise concerning ramp repairs and cleaning fees, involving approximately 8464 yuan.','After moving into Qinghe Heyuan, tenant Chen claimed that the parties disputed their oral agreement regarding ramp repairs and cleaning fees. During the handover at Qinghe Heyuan, the parties attempted to resolve the matter through the property management office but failed to reach an agreement, leading them to disagree over the timing of notice and the scope of the costs.','With respect to Qinghe Heyuan, the court reviewed the contract draft and the recording summary. These materials can establish the course of the transaction. In conjunction with the order in which the parties performed, it may be determined that key matters capable of being included in an appendix should primarily be based on written agreements, while oral statements unsupported by other evidence should not be treated as the primary basis.','The key holding is that key matters that can be included in an appendix should primarily be governed by written agreements; in the Qinghe Heyuan lease, the deposit, accessibility facilities, and repair deadline cannot be based solely on oral statements.','The Qinghe Heyuan written attachment matter was ultimately handled according to the principle of “determining liability based on valid written terms.” After calculation, the supported amount was 5824 yuan; the portion exceeding the scope of the evidence was not supported.','Mediation','Wuhan, lease, written attachment, door width');
INSERT INTO cases(case_id,case_number,title,court_id,case_type,cause,judgment_date,parties,summary,facts,reasoning,holding,ruling,outcome,keywords) VALUES ('case_wh_3522d36f','(2023) E 01 Civil First-Instance No. 20859','Qinghe Qingting Noise-Related Rent Reduction Contract Dispute Case','court_wh_intermediate','Contract Dispute','Housing lease contract dispute','2023-05-25','Tenant Tang, Landlord Jiang','Qinghe Qingting noise-related rent reduction dispute: Prolonged construction noise exceeded the reasonable tolerance level for normal living, with the disputed amount approximately 23112 yuan.','After the rental relationship at Qinghe Qingting was established, a problem arose in which “prolonged construction noise exceeded the reasonable tolerance level for normal living.” The property handover form did not record the disputed items, and the tenant and landlord subsequently each submitted evidence regarding the written attachments and actual losses.','When hearing the dispute over a rent reduction for noise at Qinghe Qingtin, the judicial panel first verified the construction notices and continuous audio recordings, then compared the contract appendices with the notification records. Based on the evidence currently available, the landlord should be held liable if they knew of the substantial impact but failed to disclose it, with liability apportioned according to the actual impact.','In the Qinghe Qingting case, the court clarified that a landlord should bear liability when it knowingly fails to disclose a material issue despite being aware of its significant impact, and that key fees and living conditions should be documented through contract appendices, handover forms, or notice records.','The court ruled regarding Qinghe Qingting: rent for the affected period shall be reduced or waived as appropriate; the established losses shall be assessed at 4559 yuan for purposes of liability, and the parties shall otherwise continue performing the contract as agreed.','Mediation','Wuhan, lease, noise-related rent reduction, security deposit');
INSERT INTO cases(case_id,case_number,title,court_id,case_type,cause,judgment_date,parties,summary,facts,reasoning,holding,ruling,outcome,keywords) VALUES ('case_wh_ca2e6a4b','(2023) E 0102 Civil First-Instance No. 22397','Guanggu Apartment Deposit Return Contract Dispute Case','court_wh_jiangan','Contract Dispute','Housing lease contract dispute','2023-09-04','Tenant Luo, Landlord Ye','Guanggu Apartment Deposit Return Dispute: The landlord withheld the deposit on the grounds of deep cleaning, involving a disputed amount of approximately 9686 yuan.','Regarding the property at Guanggu Apartment, both parties confirmed that the landlord had withheld the deposit on the grounds of deep cleaning; because the intermediary failed to provide complete records of the service process, they were unable to reach agreement on how the deposit, repair, or service costs should be borne.','The transfer records and handover photos in the Guanggu Apartment case file corroborate one another with the timeline records. On this basis, the court held that traces of normal use cannot automatically be deemed damage and strictly scrutinized commitments between the parties that were not written into the contract.','Confirmation in the Guanggu Apartment case: Traces of normal use cannot automatically be deemed damage; the issues involving door width, repairs, and service fees must all be assessed based on specific written evidence.','Regarding the Guanggu Apartment dispute, the court ordered a refund of the portion of the deposit relating to unproven losses; the amount supported by documentation was determined to be 7324 yuan, and the remaining claims were handled separately.','Mediation','Wuhan, Rental, Deposit Refund, Door Width');
INSERT INTO cases(case_id,case_number,title,court_id,case_type,cause,judgment_date,parties,summary,facts,reasoning,holding,ruling,outcome,keywords) VALUES ('case_wh_93b2bb24','(2024) Hubei 0111 Civil First Instance No. 4438','Guanggu Jiayuan Repair Liability Contract Dispute Case','court_wh_hongshan','Contract Dispute','Housing lease contract dispute','2024-11-12','Tenant Xu, Landlord Shen','Guanggu Jiayuan Repair Liability Dispute: After water seepage occurred in the bathroom, the parties disagreed over the repair deadline, with the disputed amount being approximately 12942 yuan.','This case arose from a repair-responsibility issue after the handover of Guanggu Jiayuan. The tenant submitted materials stating that, after water seepage occurred in the bathroom, the parties disagreed over the repair timeframe, while the landlord explained the handling process by stating that “the parties had previously coordinated through the property management company but failed to reach an agreement.”','For the repair-liability issue at Guanggu Jiayuan, the court treated the repair request order and on-site inspection report as the primary evidence, considered the delivery status and subsequent remedial measures, and held that the landlord should bear corresponding liability after learning of the issue but failing to make timely repairs.','The key point of the judgment is that a landlord bears corresponding liability if, after becoming aware of an issue, they fail to make repairs promptly; in the Guanggu Jiayuan lease, the deposit, accessibility facilities, and repair deadline cannot be left to oral statements alone.','The repair liability matter for Guanggu Jiayuan was ultimately handled under “bearing the repair costs and compensating reasonable losses.” Upon calculation, the supported amount is 1533 yuan; the portion exceeding the scope of the evidence is not supported.','Partial support','Wuhan, rental, repair responsibility, repairs');
INSERT INTO cases(case_id,case_number,title,court_id,case_type,cause,judgment_date,parties,summary,facts,reasoning,holding,ruling,outcome,keywords) VALUES ('case_wh_bb7299ea','(2025) E 0106 Civil First Instance No. 22997','Guanggu Lixiang Door Width Statement Contract Dispute Case','court_wh_wuchang','Contract Dispute','Housing lease contract dispute','2025-06-18','Tenant Han, landlord Lin','Guanggu Lixiang Door Width Statement Dispute: The agent orally promised a certain entrance door width, but the measurement at handover did not match, with the disputed amount being approximately 23787 yuan.','After moving into Guanggu Lixiang, tenant Han claimed that the agent had verbally promised a certain entrance-door width, but the measurement at handover did not match. During the handover at Guanggu Lixiang, the tenant promptly sent written notice after discovering the issue, leading the parties to disagree over the timing of notice and the scope of the costs.','In the matter concerning Guanggu Lixiang, the court examined chat records and photographs measuring the doorway width. These materials establish how the transaction proceeded. In conjunction with the order in which the parties performed, it may be determined that key residential conditions should be truthfully disclosed when they constitute the basis for entering into the contract, while oral assertions unsupported by other evidence should not serve as the primary basis.','In the Guanggu Lixiang case, the court held that key living conditions should be truthfully disclosed when they form the basis for entering into the contract; key fees and living conditions should be documented through contract appendices, handover forms, or notice records.','The court ruled as follows regarding Guanggu Lixiang: refund the service fee and adjust the liability for breach of contract; established losses shall be included in the liability at 3082 yuan, and the parties shall continue performing the contract with respect to the remainder.','Partial support','Wuhan, rental, door width details, accessibility');
